Z||000000||Jason Francisco||03-16-2001||08:55 PM||||I'm putting in an '86 Auto. 6 cyl in my '86 Manual 2m4.  My friend who's putting it in is concerned that the 5 speed transmission in my 2m4 may not be adequate for the 6 cyl.  Is this true? <P>------------------<BR>'86 2m4/modified<BR>Pixies rule!||||reg||1
Z||000001||skitime||03-16-2001||09:02 PM||||They use the same trans with V8s.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jason Francisco||03-16-2001||09:05 PM||||So your saying the trans. will be fine?  I won't lose power or anything?<BR>Thanks<BR>Jason<P><P>------------------<BR>'86 2m4/modified<BR>Pixies rule!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||03-16-2001||09:16 PM||||It isn't a question of loosing power.  The Isuzu 5 sp used ont he 4 cyl Fieros is not a strong as the Muncie 4 sp or Getrag 5 sp used in the V-6 cars.  The shift fork in the Isuzu is pot metal and will not tolerate many slam shift before something breaks.<P>As long as you don't speed shift the car or drop the clutch from 3,000 RPM to make tire smoke you should be OK with the Isuzu trany.<P>Below is several quotes from Ed Parks taken from the Fiero yahoo newsgroup on the suject of transmissions:<P>"The 4 spd Muncie is generally considered the strongest, the Getrag very<BR>close 2nd and the Isuzu the weakest. However, we have replaced more then<BR>10 times the Getrags vrs the 4 spd and Isuzu combined. But, then take<BR>into consideration that all Getrags are behind V6 engines and may have<BR>been driven much differently (harder)."<BR>.....<BR>"Isuzu...more evenly spaced intermediate gearing, more choices of final<BR>drive ratios (4) although stock is an "economy" ratio. Much cheaper to<BR>replace, usual cost is $250 or less. Downside: one shift fork is cast<BR>potmetal (breaks if "slam" shifted), release bearing "snout" is part of<BR>bellhousing casting (we just broke ours), shift cables are not completely<BR>compatible with V6 air intake, smaller clutch."<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
